course: dictionary
lesson: 11389
title: The abutment
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An abutment is a strong support at each end of a bridge or arch. It is made from stone, concrete or brick, and it holds the bridge up and takes its weight. «The bridge rests on two stone abutments, one at each end.»
explain: Abutments do a hard job. An arch bridge pushes outwards at its ends, and the abutments push back, so the arch does not spread apart. That is why engineers build them so strong.
explain: The word can also mean a place where two things touch or join. «The shed was built with its back wall at the abutment, where it met the house.» Look at the words around it: bridges and supports point to the first meaning, while touching and joining point to the second.
fact: An abutment is a strong structure at each end of a bridge or arch.
fact: Abutments are often made of stone, concrete or brick.
fact: Abutments hold up the bridge and take its weight.
fact: On an arch bridge, the abutments push back so the arch does not spread apart.
fact: «Abutment» can also mean the place where two things touch or join.
exercise: «The bridge rests on two stone abutments, one at each end.» What is an abutment? | A kind of rope used to tie boats | *A strong structure that holds up a bridge at its end | A small boat that sails under the bridge | A long flat road across the river
exercise: «The arch bridge was held up by its abutments, which pushed back against the arch.» Why do the abutments push back? | To make the bridge taller | To stop boats from sailing past | To make the water flow faster | *So the arch does not spread apart
exercise: «The shed was built with its back wall at the abutment, where it met the house.» What does abutment mean here? | A kind of window | A small garden tool | A heavy stone that holds up a bridge | *The place where two things touch or join
test: «Engineers built stone abutments at each end of the new bridge.» What are the abutments for? | To make the bridge lighter | *To hold up the bridge and take its weight | To keep river water out of the town | To make the bridge look pretty
test: «The two pieces of wood met at a tight abutment.» What does abutment mean here? | *The place where they touch and join | A tool for cutting | A heavy support for a bridge | A type of wood
exam: Which of these best describes an abutment on a bridge? | *A strong structure at each end that holds up the bridge | A kind of bird that nests under arches | A long piece of rope that stops the bridge swinging | A bright paint colour used on bridges
